The user just asked how to make the game run faster. I’ve already addressed the biggest performance culprits and started applying concrete optimizations to the code. I’ve already:

1. Reduced the renderer pixel ratio cap to 1.5 and disabled automatic shadow updates every other frame.
2. Halved the shadow map resolution and narrowed the shadow camera frustum.
3. Added a note about the hidden preview workflow.

Now I need to tackle the next major source of load: the trees. In the original `index.html`, the tree creation code builds a single massive InstancedMesh for all trunks, branches, blobs (inner foliage), and leaf cards. This means every tree is always rendered, even those behind the camera, and the leaf cards are also rendered into the shadow map, which is expensive. My earlier plan outlined “sector the trees” – split them into angular sectors and distance rings, cull whole groups that are out of view, reduce leaf cards for far trees, and avoid rendering leaf cards into the shadow pass.

To implement that, I must replace the existing block of code that creates the tree meshes. I’ll locate the exact `old_string` that currently defines the whole tree generation: it starts with the bark texture, builds `trunkGeo`, applies irregularity, creates `trunkMat`, then creates an InstancedMesh for trunks, then builds branches, blobs, and cards—all in one go. I need to replace that entire block with a new version that:

- Keeps the geometry and material definitions (bark texture, trunk geometry, branch geometry, leaf texture, blob geometry/material, card geometry/material) unchanged.
- Adds a sectoring system: define a constant number of angular sectors (`SECTORS = 12`) and a near-ring distance (`NEAR_RING = 160`). Create an array of groups (`groups`) sized `SECTORS * 2` (near and far rings). Loop over `treePositions` and assign each tree to a sector based on its angle (`Math.atan2(p.z, p.x)`) and distance (`Math.hypot(p.x, p.z)`). This will give us two sets per sector: near and far.
- For each non‑empty group, decide whether it’s “far” (second ring) and adjust the per‑tree instance counts: fewer leaf cards (`cardsPer = far ? 7 : 16`), no branches for far trees (`branchesPer = far ? 0 : 3`), and keep the same number of blobs (`blobsPer = 3`).
- Create InstancedMesh objects per group: trunks, optional branches, blobs, and cards. Set `castShadow` appropriately (e.g., only trunks and blobs cast shadows when not far; leaf cards never cast shadows). Populate each mesh’s matrices using the same randomization logic as before but scoped to the group’s trees.
- Compute bounding spheres for each mesh so that the renderer can cull entire groups when they’re outside the camera frustum.
- Add each mesh to the scene.

I need to be careful to preserve variable names used later (`q`, `m`, `v`, `sV`, `blobColor`) and to keep the injection of sway for materials unchanged. Also, the new code must still reference `treePositions`, which is defined earlier in the file.

